"""

What is Analysis?
Analysis in computer science refers to the systematic examination and evaluation of algorithms and data structures to understand their efficiency, performance, and resource usage. It involves studying how algorithms behave with different inputs and how they scale with increasing data sizes.

Types of Analysis:
1. Time Complexity: Measures the amount of time an algorithm takes to complete as a function of the input size. Common notations include Big O, Big Theta, and Big Omega.
2. Space Complexity: Measures the amount of memory an algorithm uses in relation to the input size
3. Amortized Analysis: Evaluates the average time per operation over a sequence of operations, providing a more accurate measure for algorithms with varying operation costs.
4. Worst-case, Average-case, and Best-case Analysis: Different scenarios to evaluate an algorithm's performance based on input conditions.
Examples of Analysis:
1. Analyzing the time complexity of a sorting algorithm like Quick Sort, which has an average-case time complexity of O(n log n) and a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2).
2. Evaluating the space complexity of a recursive algorithm, such as calculating Fibonacci numbers, which has a space complexity of O(n) due to the call stack.


Analysis Type : 
1. Apriori Analysis: This type of analysis is performed before the execution of the algorithm. It involves analyzing the algorithm's structure, logic, and design to predict its behavior and performance. Apriori analysis helps in understanding the theoretical aspects of the algorithm and can be used to identify potential bottlenecks or inefficiencies.
2. Post-hoc Analysis: This type of analysis is performed after the execution of the algorithm. It involves analyzing the actual performance and behavior of the algorithm based on empirical data collected during its execution. Post-hoc analysis helps in understanding how the algorithm performs in real-world scenarios and can be used to identify areas for optimization or improvement.
3. Dynamic Analysis: This type of analysis is performed during the execution of the algorithm. It involves monitoring and analyzing the algorithm's behavior in real-time, often using tools and techniques such as profiling and debugging. Dynamic analysis helps in understanding how the algorithm interacts with the system and can be used to identify runtime issues or inefficiencies.
4. Static Analysis: This type of analysis is performed without executing the algorithm. It involves analyzing the code and structure of the algorithm to identify potential issues, such as syntax errors, logical errors, or security vulnerabilities. Static analysis can be performed using automated tools or manual code reviews.
5. Complexity Analysis: This type of analysis focuses on evaluating the time and space complexity of an algorithm. It involves analyzing how the algorithm's performance scales with increasing input sizes and identifying the factors that contribute to its efficiency or inefficiency.
6. Empirical Analysis: This type of analysis involves collecting and analyzing data from the execution of the algorithm to evaluate its performance. It can include measuring execution time, memory usage, and other relevant metrics to assess the algorithm's efficiency in practice.
7. Theoretical Analysis: This type of analysis involves using mathematical techniques and theoretical frameworks to analyze the properties and behavior of an algorithm. It can include proving the correctness of the algorithm, analyzing its asymptotic behavior, and deriving bounds on its performance.

"""
